ise every worker in this country  is struggling at the moment they can't  keep up with the utility prices they  can't keep up with their household bills  we need a pay rise but we also need job  security because there's a lot of  precarious work and vulnerable work at  the moment and that's got to end if  you're saying that everyone deserves a  pay rise you're not worried about the  impact that this could have on inflation  which is you know the very thing that  everyone's really concerned about you  know the bank of england governor andrew  bailey calling for pay restraint because  his pay restraint he's on 600 000 pounds  a year as is the chief of network rail  there are railway bosses taking home  millions of pounds every year the  railways made 500 million pound of  profit last year when uh fares and  passengers were at an all-time low  people are stripping money  out of the railway they're stripping  money out of the economy and if workers  wages don't go up it means a transfer of  wealth from the poor to the rich we've  got more billionaires than we've ever  had in this country the rich have never  been richer and the reason they're able  to do that and get richer all the time  is because they're deliberately  depressing workers wages inflation has  come on board now and virtually nobody's  had a pay rise for the last two or three  years so this idea that there's a wages  price spiral is nonsense i totally get  um  and i'm sure many of our viewers will  share you know your assessment of the  very very high wages particularly the  high wages of those people calling for  pay restraint  but doesn't change the facts though does  it that if if lots of people get pay  rises and it could lead to inflation  well inflation is already here and  nobody said more inflation well it could  lead to more inflation but what could  also stop inflation is that some of the  companies could restrain their profits  and return that money into lower prices  bp and shell on the others are making  record profits of billions of pounds  every quarter and everyone yearly they  don't got so much money they don't know  what to do with it one of the things  they could do is sacrifice those profits  never mind a windfall tax what about a  dividend that goes back to the people  that are consuming their their materials  but also we've got thousands and  thousands if not millions of people who  are struggling and while they're working  and having to take benefits that's an  outrage the reason they have to take  benefits is they're not being paid  enough  and they need to be paid more okay  now just talking about the strike action  do you think it's now inevitable  well we're talking to the companies  we're talking to the companies at a very  senior level but they are taking a very  hard line they want to impose these cuts  while they're imposing record increases  and fares  their uh fare increases are linked to  the retail price index  wages on the question is do you think  the strikes are inevitable i can't see a  way out from the strikes at the moment  unless there's a breakthrough unless the  government instructs these companies  which they are doing to to change their  line rather than harden their line it's  very very likely that they'll be strike  action and it will be very soon and what  what level of pay increase would you  like to see for your members well i'll  negotiate that with the companies  there's a lot of ways to put value into  a package just give us a rough idea well  we want to see something that reflects  the ever-increasing costs our  anniversaries for these companies were  back in december and back in  april so does that mean you'd like to  see it roughly the same levels as  inflation well it needs to meet the  aspirations of our members which  coincide with inflation yes so roughly  around the level that inflation  currently is well that's what they need  to do to keep up otherwise our people  become poorer date to 10  well we'll talk to the companies about  that okay  now millions of people across the  country are gearing up to  make good use of the extra bank holidays  to celebrate the queen's  jubilee and and yet their plan strikes  on the london underground  well there's not we called off a strike  next friday we've got a way forward on  that which is on one of the public  holidays so there's no strike action by  the rmt over any of the queen's jubilee  celebrations on both the june the 3rd  and june the 6th well june 6 is after  the celebration okay but it's still on  the back holiday though isn't it well  the bank holds thursday and friday this  is a normal work day on the monday  okay right so you all right okay that's  where we are  you've you've you've caught off one of  the strike actions but how how worried  are you about the disruption that it  could cause people and the sympathy that  you might get well we don't want  disruption it's a hard road being a  trade unionist and taking industrial  action but we feel we have no choice the  london mayor is attacking our people  he's attacking their pensions he wants  to make them poorer permanently not just  while they're at work but while they're  in retirement it's exactly the same  agenda that the government's putting  forward through these funding  negotiations with the companies and with  the london mayor we cannot sit idly by  as a trade unionist while while our  people are being attacked  while they're at work while they finish  when they finish work for retirement and  very threat that they may lose their  jobs it's my job as a trade union leader  to defend our people and this is a  defensive strike if it happens their  tanks are on our lawn we're not  threatening them they're threatening our  people you're looking ahead to the  summer do you think we could see you  know a summer of discontent well there  are a lot of workers that are very upset  i'm seeing that there's action going on  in the local councils we've got refuse  workers we've got care workers  most people who come from traditional  working class environments find  themselves super exploited not only they  got poor wages they've got poor  conditions if they've got any conditions  but they also find themselves under the  threat of cuts and that being job cuts  so there has to be a response from all  of the trade unions and from the tuc and  we would like to see the labour party  and its leadership coming behind working  class people so that they ensure they  get a pay rise so there's some support  from the politicians as well as from the  trade unions and this this is a measure  for kia stammer so that he can decide  whether he's on the side of the workers  in this country or on the side of the  bosses do you think that he hasn't made  that clear then he hasn't made it clear  at all i've not heard him say once  uh during the current dispute such as at  the coventry uh  council where the labour council sought  to cut workers wages and cut their  conditions i've seen no response from  the labour front bench that says  we support the workers in their  struggles and that is the role of a  labour party  the name gives it away that there's a  put they're there to support the labour  movement do you think that kirsten was  on the side of workers i can't see you  at the moment  wow and so what do you think that means  for the you know the funding of the  labour party and the union support for  the load plan well my union's not  affiliated with the labour party but i  see many other unions general  secretaries and leaders thinking what is  the point of this connection if we just  get this bland democratic party sitting  in the center of  politics  taking their their instructions off the  daily mail to some extent and not  actually getting behind workers  struggles you have to ask yourself why  do they call themselves the labor party  so we need to see a strong line the  labour party needs to get back into  working class communities support better  wages and support the end of precarious  work which is outsourced subcontracted  and vulnerable work where you don't know  where you're going to get your next  week's work from in terms of these  flexible hours contracts and zero hours  contracts and all the rest of it so they  have to make a stand for working people  and i guess what kirsten would say is  that he's looked at the last uh election  results when there was a more left-wing  leader and it was a catastrophic set of  results for the labour party and that  it's hit up to him to try and re-engage  with some of those people who turned  away from labour yeah some of those  people have turned away from labour are  the people who have not got any decent  job security in this economy now if he  wants to go back to the red wall and now  the blue wall he's got to make a  connection with working people and one  of those things is about  the fact that there's no decent housing  being built by councils the fact that  people cannot rely on an income and the  fact that they're underpaid and this is  a crisis and it's time for kierstarma to  stand up for our people okay just uh  finally um you have previously said that  you can't rule out four-day uh strikes  are you concerned about what a four-day  strike could look like for this country  so you know for example you know if they  could lead to even the lights going out  because of disruption to services drax  power station north yorkshire servicing  millions of homes and there's only a  certain amount  of uh  of  power that it can stockpile are you  concerned i'm concerned about disruption  i don't want disruption and nor do my  members but they don't want to see their  conditions stripped out and they don't  want to live under an ever-present  threat of job cuts  network rail have already announced that  two and a half hours of my people will  be made redundant they've already made  1500 job cuts in that company and that's  the first installment now we're going to  see a stripped out dehumanized railway  if we're not careful if the companies  want to come back to the table and work  with us to get a proper settlement we  can do that very quickly and it wouldn't  need to be any strikes at all do you  think it genuinely could lead to the  lights going up i've got no idea it  depends how long the strike goes on if  there is strike action then it's up to  the government to give a mandate to the  companies that they can settle this  dispute how long could the strikes be  they could go on for a very very long  time because there's no sign at the  moment that anybody's backing down on  their side of the table  well we'll decide that as we go we want  to make the strike action if it happens  as effective as possible from our point  of view but just you are prepared to  strike for long enough that it could  affect the power supply if you don't get  you to have your demands matters our  members are prepared to take effective  strike action in pursuit of the  settlement of this dispute i've got no  idea how long that will take and i can't  determine from here what what the  outcomes and side effects of that will  be

